---
id: AP-015
type: rule
family: type-system-evasion
title: Zod schema escape hatch (z.any / .passthrough)
version: 1

severity: warning
confidence: high
spectrum_position: 2

targets: [source]
file_extensions: [.ts, .tsx, .js, .jsx, .mjs, .cjs]
allowlist: ['*.d.ts', '**/__mocks__/**', '**/test/**/*.ts', '**/__tests__/**', '**/*.setup.ts', '**/*.setup.js', '**/explain/**']

detection:
  type: regex
  pattern: '\bz\.any\s*\(|\bz\.[^;]*\.passthrough\s*\('

related: [AP-003, AP-016]
enabled: true
opt_in: false
---

Don't reach for a Zod escape hatch here. `z.any()` accepts any value
without validating it, and `.passthrough()` lets unvalidated keys flow
through untouched — each one punches a hole in the very schema you
reached for to close. A Zod schema that bottoms out in `z.any()` gives
the caller the same false confidence as `: any` (AP-003): the type says
"validated", the runtime validates nothing.

If the field has a known shape, model it — `z.object({ ... })`,
`z.string()`, `z.enum([...])`, a discriminated union. If only part of
the value is load-bearing, validate that part and narrow the rest behind
a type guard rather than passing the whole value through unchecked.

For unknown keys, `.strict()` (reject them) or an explicit
`.catchall(schema)` (validate them) is almost always the contract you
want instead of `.passthrough()`.

The `.passthrough()` arm is anchored to a Zod receiver on the same line
(`z.…().passthrough(`) and the span cannot cross a `;`, so a
`.passthrough()` method on a non-Zod object (streams, mocks) does not
false-fire — at the cost of missing a `.passthrough()` chained onto its
own line. Comments and string literals are masked (the rule is
code-scoped), so a `z.any(` mentioned in prose is not flagged. The
detector is still line-based and not AST-aware: aliased imports
(`import { z as zod }`), bracket notation (`z["any"]()`), and
destructured calls (`const { any } = z; any()`) are not distinguished.
Use the standard suppression directive or the per-file allowlist for the
rare residual false positive.

`z.unknown()` is handled separately by the opt-in rule AP-016 — it is
the least-bad of the escape hatches (consumers must still narrow) and is
idiomatic as a leaf in a typed record (`z.record(z.string(),
z.unknown())`), so it is off by default.
